WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.980
If Ras must carry a farnesyl chain to work, block the enzyme that attaches it.

2
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.320
That was the idea behind farnesyltransferase inhibitors.

3
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:20.000
Ras genes are mutated in roughly one in five human cancers.

4
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:24.340
A Ras that cannot reach the membrane cannot signal.

5
00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:30.653
One of these drugs, tipifarnib, reached Phase III clinical trials.

6
00:00:31.713 --> 00:00:33.553
The drugs worked as drugs.

7
00:00:33.893 --> 00:00:38.033
Yet they still disappointed against the Ras that matters most.

8
00:00:39.413 --> 00:00:43.853
K-Ras is by far the most commonly mutated of the three Ras genes.

9
00:00:45.033 --> 00:00:46.153
H-Ras is the least.

10
00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:48.607
Here is the catch.

11
00:00:49.407 --> 00:00:55.527
When farnesylation is blocked, K-Ras and N-Ras can be picked up by a second enzyme.

12
00:00:56.227 --> 00:00:59.087
It is geranylgeranyltransferase I.

13
00:00:59.747 --> 00:01:04.787
It puts a geranylgeranyl group, a longer chain, on the same cysteine.

14
00:01:05.467 --> 00:01:08.227
The protein keeps its anchor, and keeps signaling.

15
00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.910
H-Ras is not rescued this way.

16
00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:13.950
The logic has not died.

17
00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:19.030
Tipifarnib has been tested in cancers driven by mutant H-Ras.

18
00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:28.030
So keep this rule: block farnesyl, and K-Ras and N-Ras can switch anchors, but H-Ras cannot.
